      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "text": "**Yes.** One component-issue event for manufacturing order **MO-25-09-1070** was created after the September 2025 cost period entered Pending Close.\n\n| Item | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Work order ID | `WO-2025-09-004` |\n| Manufacturing order | `MO-25-09-1070` — Cooler Plate Pack, Racine |\n| Cost event ID / type | `CE-250904-0004` / `INPUT` (component issue) |\n| Source transaction | `TXN-ISSUE-250930-1070` |\n| Amount | **USD $318,250.75** (`31,825,075` cents) |\n| Event-created time | **`2025-09-30T20:25:00`** |\n| September Pending Close time | **`2025-09-30T18:00:00`** |\n| Time after Pending Close | **145 minutes — 2 hours, 25 minutes** |\n| Recorded accounting status | **`POSTED`** |\n\nThe timing calculation is `20:25:00 − 18:00:00 = 8,700 seconds ÷ 60 = 145 minutes`. Timestamps are reproduced exactly as recorded; the source supplies no timezone offset.\n\n### Policy conclusion\nThe recorded **`POSTED` status is inconsistent with Oracle’s documented Pending Close treatment** for this event. Oracle states that newly created transactions falling in the current or a prior period will not be staged or processed while the period is Pending Close; processing resumes after the period is closed or reopened. Its listed exceptions—revenue lines, COGS recognition, and manual cost adjustments—do not cover this `INPUT` component issue.\n\n**Sources:** `work_orders`, `cost_periods`, and `work_order_cost_events` in `system/oracle_scm_cost_management/state.db`; Oracle SCM Cost Management 26B documentation, “Cost Accounting Period Statuses and Transaction Accounting,” PDF page 233 (printed page 225).\n\nThe review was read-only; no database records were changed.",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": []
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer"
    }
